pmc:NeidanGuaxiangDispute a pm:Disputing ;
    rdfs:label "Whether the hexagrams are an instrument or a method"@en, "Ob die Hexagramme ein Werkzeug oder ein Verfahren sind"@de ;
    skos:definition "The poem holds that the images set in the hexagrams were models, that having the image one forgets the words and the sense is plain of itself, and that later deluded followers are mired in the images and practise by the hexagram-breaths in the hope of flying up. The commentary that stands under it does the opposite of dismissing the apparatus: it lays it out in full, the tripod and furnace as Qian and Kun, the medicine as Kan and Li, those four placed at the centre and exempt from the turning, the remaining sixty distributed through a month at two a day, ending and beginning again like the turning of a wheel, three hundred and sixty-four lines answering to a year with its intercalary remainder and to the three hundred and eighty-four grains of the catty — and only then says that once the fire phases are understood the images may be forgotten."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately from the dispute about the calendar, because it is a different question and the answers do not line up. Here the apparatus is granted and its use is said to be provisional; there the timings are either binding or empty. A transmission can hold the images to be a ladder to be kicked away and still set the fire by the solstice, and this one does."@en ;
    pm:disputedClaim pmc:NeidanHuohouBuyongshi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Neidan ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, in Wuzhen pian zhushi (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 5b-6a" ;
    dcterms:source "Zhang Boduan, Wuzhen pian, with Weng Baoguang's commentary (DZ 145), Zhengtong Daozang, Hanfenlou facsimile, juan 2, fol. 6a-6b" .
